1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,550
Hello, I'm Satyam Pandey, and this is GridResolve AI,

2
00:00:04,550 --> 00:00:08,800
evidence-first utility billing resolution, built on Microsoft Foundry.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,700
A customer says their bill jumped, so the meter must be broken.

4
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:15,700
GridResolve investigates instead of agreeing.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,100
I orchestrated nine GPT-5 agents in a hosted Foundry workflow.

6
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:23,100
Four investigate, one plans, one drafts,

7
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:27,500
and three handle independent compliance, human escalation, and audit.

8
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:33,700
In the final synthetic case, usage rose from 640 to 870 kilowatt hours.

9
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:36,100
The meter readings reconciled to the bill.

10
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:41,600
The evidence agent produced 22 traceable entries, and the policy agent mapped nine policies.

11
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:43,800
None establishes meter failure.

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,600
My Control Center visualizes claim provenance

13
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:51,300
and separates actual Foundry execution from the offline demonstration.

14
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:56,400
Every conclusion can be traced back to its supporting evidence and applicable policy.

15
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,800
Evidence Compliance Agent independently reviews the draft and records its reasons.

16
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:05,500
A Power Fx gate requires an exact approval token,

17
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:09,000
complete investigations, and six readable message fields.

18
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:11,800
Human case follow-up remains a separate decision.

19
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:14,600
This separation actually matters.

20
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:18,300
Approving a customer explanation never authorizes a financial adjustment,

21
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:22,800
confirms a defective meter, or closes an unresolved investigation.

22
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:28,900
Early Foundry runs exposed defects: a literal release expression and stalled agents,

23
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:33,300
and traced the stalls to missing user turns in shared conversation history,

24
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:35,700
and corrected every agent handoff.

25
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:41,300
Workflow version 10 then passed all 14 pre-registered hosted acceptance criteria.

26
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:43,600
All nine agents completed.

27
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,850
Compliance approved the supported message without promising a credit.

28
00:01:46,850 --> 00:01:52,200
The workflow released readable prose and prepared a Billing Supervisor review package.

29
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:54,600
The audit matched the platform evidence.

30
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,200
Another 1,174 local checks and 105 Power Fx cases passed.

31
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:05,200
Foundry tracing and Azure Monitor reconciled token usage.

32
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:10,400
Three runs cost about 13 cents by token estimate, pending billing confirmation.

33
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:14,500
Beyond the hosted workflow, I built eight typed integration ports,

34
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:18,700
synthetic adapters, and locally tested Microsoft Agent Framework migration.

35
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:24,400
I also prepared 30 evaluation cases and 16 adversarial scenarios for a broader validation.

36
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:29,900
The next milestone is connecting these validated interfaces to real utility systems,

37
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:35,200
completing operational security testing, and piloting the workflow with billing supervisors.

38
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:38,400
GridResolve AI: evidence before explanation,

39
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:43,300
independent review before release, and human authority over consequential decisions.

40
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:45,400
Thank you for watching.
